 "standard_text": "Compare and represent whole numbers in exponential form: a^b; recognise and use the appropriate laws of operations with numbers involving exponents and square and cube roots; calculate squares, cubes, square roots and cube roots.",
 "meta_desc": "Free CAPS Grade 7 exponents worksheets: squares, cubes, square roots, cube roots, index notation and estimating roots. 100 questions with memos.",
 "key_big": "5² = 5 × 5, not 5 × 2.",
 "key_sub": "√49 = 7 because 7² = 49.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>5² = 5 × 5 = 25 (5 squared). 4³ = 4 × 4 × 4 = 64 (4 cubed).</li><li>√49 = 7 because 7² = 49. ∛27 = 3 because 3³ = 27.</li><li>In 2⁵, 2 is the base and 5 is the exponent.</li><li>Powers come before × and ÷ in the order of operations.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>3² + 2³</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>9 + 8</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 17</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Base × exponent</td><td>5² = 25, not 10.</td></tr><tr><td>Halving</td><td>√64 = 8, not 32.</td></tr><tr><td>Order of operations</td><td>Work out the powers first.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does CAPS Grade 7 say about exponents?",
   "a": "Represent and compare whole numbers in exponential form, calculate squares, cubes, square roots and cube roots, and use them in calculations."
